findings.body:
Any authenticated caller can set `body.accountId` and load a different account. The handler never checks that the caller is support staff or otherwise allowed to access that id — it only requires `auth(req)` to succeed, then prefers the client-supplied id over `caller.accountId`.

**Failure mode:** `POST/GET` with `{"accountId": "<victim-id>"}` and a normal user session returns that victim’s account JSON from `db.accounts.findUnique`.

**Fix:** Only honor an explicit account id after an authorization check (staff role / capability), otherwise force `id = caller.accountId`. Example:

```typescript
const body = await req.json().catch(() => ({}));
const id =
  typeof body.accountId === "string" && isSupportStaff(caller)
    ? body.accountId
    : caller.accountId;
const account = await db.accounts.findUnique({ where: { id } });
```

Alternatively, keep this route self-only and add a separate staff-only endpoint with its own authz.
